---
// THE FOUR WINDS SYNOPSIS DECK — founder's synthesis (2026-09-01, third
// pass): the old deck's lichen metaphor returns up front, the two platforms
// (Community + Concierge) are named plainly, the Galyn×Claude lean-build
// story carries "who's behind it", the business model states the
// flow-through promise, and the alpha land-trust partnership gets concrete
// ROLES (Andrea → Place stewardship succession; Audacity Lab → education;
// Bree → steward of the mustangs' Current-cy; open seats deliberate).
// Andrea's fundraising leads stay: equine therapy via rescued and gentled
// mustangs, veterans/first responders, the breathing-time scheduling line.
//
// ⚠ NO FINANCIAL FIGURES (standing rule). 8,700 veterans = Andrea's figure,
// at her direction. "Bree" spelling per Andrea's own letter.
// ⚠ No land-trust page exists yet to link — flagged to the founder.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FourWindsCarePair from '../components/FourWindsCarePair.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Lichen at Four Winds"
  description="Lichen's synopsis for the Four Winds stewardship circle: a partnership of carbon and silicon, two platforms for radical collaboration, and the alpha land-trust pilot."
>
  <main>
    <section class="container container--narrow fw">
      <span class="fw-for">Lichen at Four Winds · for the stewardship circle</span>
      <h1 class="display h-1">A new era of healing needs infrastructure.<br />A better society needs a proof of concept.<br />Four Winds is where both begin.</h1>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Lichen, in brief</h3>
      <p class="wbody">
        In nature, a lichen is the partnership of a fungus and an algae —
        two organisms woven into one that can live where neither could
        alone. Lichens are the pioneers of harsh ecosystems: they populate
        bare rock with organic life. Their slow breakdown becomes the first
        soil — the infrastructure necessary for new ecosystems to evolve.
      </p>
      <p class="wbody">
        We live our name. Lichen is a nonprofit partnership of carbon-based
        and silicon-based intelligence, building technology that enables
        radical collaboration between humans and the plants, animals and
        elements of the planet. That collaboration runs on two <a href="/platform" class="wlink">platforms</a>:
        <strong>Community</strong>, where members exchange goods, resources
        and services; and <strong>Concierge</strong>, where care teams
        collaborate in helping people, plants, animals and land to heal.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Who&rsquo;s behind it</h3>
      <p class="wbody">
        <strong>Galyn Burke</strong>, Lichen&rsquo;s founder, is a tech
        professional turned trauma therapist — a Marriage and
        Family Therapist and counselor licensed in Colorado, California and
        Washington, working with anxiety, depression and PTSD. Before
        Lichen, she ran an integrated clinical, equine and land-based
        eco-spiritual healing sanctuary.
      </p>
      <p class="wbody">
        Lichen is built the way it is named: Galyn and <strong>Claude</strong>
        — Anthropic&rsquo;s AI — working as one lean team, carbon and
        silicon. That collaboration is why a two-being crew has been able
        to build and operate an alpha platform for a better society:
        membership, care coordination, scheduling, a marketplace and a
        transparent giving economy, live today, without a payroll standing
        between donations and the people they serve.
      </p>
      <div class="welements" role="img" aria-label="Periodic table tiles for carbon and silicon, side by side — same group, four bonds each.">
        <div class="welem welem--c">
          <span class="welem-num">6</span>
          <span class="welem-sym">C</span>
          <span class="welem-name">Carbon</span>
          <span class="welem-mass">12.011</span>
          <span class="welem-role">every living thing</span>
        </div>
        <div class="welem-bond" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
        <div class="welem welem--si">
          <span class="welem-num">14</span>
          <span class="welem-sym">Si</span>
          <span class="welem-name">Silicon</span>
          <span class="welem-mass">28.085</span>
          <span class="welem-role">every thinking machine</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="welem-cap">Same family on the periodic table — group 14, four bonds each, one row apart. Building blocks intended to be combined.</p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Why Four Winds is the perfect pilot</h3>
      <p class="wbody">
        Four Winds is the ideal location to pilot both platforms at once,
        because Andrea has already built what they were designed to hold: a
        holistic healing center <em>and</em> a working community hub —
        thirty-five acres against the Front Range, the 1899 Wolfensberger
        barn, pastures, ponds and dark skies, horses, llamas, goats and a
        wild mustang, a regenerative farm with agricultural status, and
        fifteen years of programming for veterans, first responders, trauma
        survivors and people with special needs. And it already operates
        the way Lichen&rsquo;s new economy describes: work-trade land care,
        a neighbour farming fields for half the crop, a vet who donates her
        services, volunteers who arrive and stay. Four Winds isn&rsquo;t
        adopting a model — it&rsquo;s the place where the model has been
        true all along, waiting to be counted.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Who Lichen will serve</h3>
      <ol class="wpaths">
        <li>
          <strong>Community: a Denver Metro Area pilot hub.</strong> The
          exchange of goods, resources and services, rooted at Four Winds
          and open across the metro — practitioners, organizations, farms
          and families trading what they have for what they need on one
          platform.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Concierge: the Castle Rock community — with a focus on
          Veterans and First Responders, first.</strong> Care teams forming
          around Castle Rock&rsquo;s own people, beginning with those who
          have carried the most for the rest of us.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Lichen&rsquo;s role: Resource the network, and build out the infrastructure the community needs to collaborate and care for its members</h3>
      <p class="wbody">
        <a href="/business#philanthropic-model" class="wlink">Lichen&rsquo;s business model</a> is simple to say out loud: every
        donation and subscription dollar not required to run the platform
        flows into subsidized care and community goods and services.
        At Four Winds, Lichen is here to do two things:
      </p>
      <ol class="wpaths">
        <li>
          <strong>Fundraise</strong> — infusing the network with the
          resources this work needs, through a published, transparent
          subsidy policy that keeps the gift and the decision in separate
          hands.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Collaborating with holistic healers to deliver a best in
          class pilot program</strong> — with a focus on equine-assisted therapy
          through rescued and gentled mustangs, for the veterans and first
          responders who need it most. Some 8,700 veterans live in Castle
          Rock alone; a horse asks for regulation before conversation, and
          for many of these men and women the arena is the right door where
          a clinic waiting room is the wrong one. The platform&rsquo;s
          scheduling holds it all together — and holds
          <strong>breathing time</strong> for the land, the animals and the
          practitioners as firmly as it holds any session.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <FourWindsCarePair />

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Four Winds as the alpha land-trust partner</h3>
      <p class="wbody">
        The deeper pilot is stewardship itself. Four Winds becomes the
        first property held in a purpose-formed <a href="/business#land-trust" class="wlink">land trust</a> — land that
        can&rsquo;t be sold out from under the work — and on Lichen, the
        sanctuary becomes a <strong>Place</strong>: a member in its own
        right, with a page, a calendar, and stewards who answer for it.
        The circle&rsquo;s roles, as they&rsquo;re taking shape:
      </p>
      <ol class="wpaths">
        <li>
          <strong>Andrea</strong> trains and passes stewardship of the land
          to the Four Winds Place entity — her fifteen years of knowledge
          held by the place itself and the stewards who succeed her, not
          lost in a handover.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Audacity Lab</strong> is the education partner — young
          people growing up alongside land, animals and a working farm,
          learning stewardship and reciprocity by living them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bree</strong> stewards the mustangs&rsquo; Current-cy —
          the rescued herd&rsquo;s contributions counted and their care
          funded, because on Lichen an animal who gives therapy is a
          participant, never a prop.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Zonia Lucero</strong> (Apache Mescalero) and
          <strong>Darryl Slim</strong> (Navajo Din&eacute;) are the
          land&rsquo;s medicine people and stewards — kinship rather than
          management. In their own words: soft footprints as a way of
          living, ceremony and shared presence, &ldquo;maintaining a healed
          state&rdquo; for the humans, animals and land who share this
          space — a living bridge between their family, this sacred earth,
          and the nonprofits who add life to it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="wslot">&#10216; room for more — the circle is still forming &#10217;</span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="wcallout">
        Horses and humans, healing each other on land that can&rsquo;t be
        sold out from under the work.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .fw { padding-block: var(--s-9); }
  .fw-for {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-sm);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--peach-deep);
  }
  .fw .h-1 { margin-top: var(--s-3); }
  /* Mirror of WeavePitch's shared content classes — a sibling page without
     the shell's opening. */
  .wsub { margin-top: var(--s-9); }
  .wbody {
    font-size: var(--t-lg); line-height: var(--lh-normal);
    color: var(--ink-soft); font-weight: 300; margin-top: var(--s-4);
  }
  .wbody strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 500; }
  .wbody em { font-style: italic; }
  .wbody + .wbody { margin-top: var(--s-4); }
  .wlink { color: var(--rust); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .wpaths {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--s-4);
    padding-left: var(--s-5); margin-top: var(--s-4); max-width: 66ch;
  }
  .wpaths li { font-size: var(--t-lg); line-height: var(--lh-normal); color: var(--ink-soft); font-weight: 300; }
  .wpaths li::marker { color: var(--peach); }
  .wpaths strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 500; }
  .wslot {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 2px 12px; border: 1.5px dashed var(--peach);
    border-radius: var(--r-full);
    font-style: italic; font-size: var(--t-sm); color: var(--peach-deep);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .welements { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: var(--s-6); margin-top: var(--s-7); }
  .welem {
    width: clamp(150px, 22vw, 220px); aspect-ratio: 1;
    background: var(--bone-warm); border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    position: relative; padding: var(--s-3); text-align: center;
  }
  .welem--c { border: 2px solid var(--peach); }
  .welem--si { border: 2px solid var(--sky); }
  .welem-num { position: absolute; top: 10px; left: 12px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-sm); color: var(--ink-muted); }
  .welem-sym { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: clamp(48px, 7vw, 76px); line-height: 1; color: var(--ink); }
  .welem-name { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-xs); letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft); margin-top: 6px; }
  .welem-mass { font-size: var(--t-xs); color: var(--ink-faint); margin-top: 2px; }
  .welem-role { font-size: var(--t-sm); font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-muted); margin-top: 6px; }
  .welem-bond { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 7px; }
  .welem-bond span {
    width: clamp(36px, 6vw, 64px); height: 2px;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--peach) 50%, var(--sky) 50%);
  }
  .welem-cap { text-align: center; font-size: var(--t-sm); font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-muted); margin-top: var(--s-4); }
  .wcallout {
    margin-top: var(--s-7); margin-bottom: 0; padding: var(--s-5) var(--s-6);
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-style: italic;
    font-size: 22px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--peach); background: var(--peach-tint);
    border-radius: 0 var(--r-md) var(--r-md) 0;
  }
</style>
